    {
      "heading": "Brackets: the index goes to everything",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "In 2 x cubed y, all to the 4, the 4 goes to every factor.",
          "write": "(2x³y)⁴"
        },
        {
          "say": "2 to the 4 is 16. x cubed to the 4 is x to the 12.",
          "write": "= 2⁴ × (x³)⁴ × y⁴",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 260,
            "alt": "The outside index 4 goes to the 2, x cubed and y",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 150,
                "text": "(2 x³ y)⁴",
                "size": 60,
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 30,
                "text": "the 4 goes to each factor",
                "size": 18,
                "change": "add",
                "color": "red"
              },
              {
                "kind": "arrow",
                "x": 200,
                "y": 45,
                "x2": 200,
                "y2": 95,
                "color": "red",
                "change": "add"
              },
              {
                "kind": "arrow",
                "x": 262,
                "y": 45,
                "x2": 262,
                "y2": 95,
                "color": "red",
                "change": "add"
              },
              {
                "kind": "arrow",
                "x": 332,
                "y": 45,
                "x2": 332,
                "y2": 95,
                "color": "red",
                "change": "add"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 230,
                "text": "2⁴ × (x³)⁴ × y⁴",
                "size": 26,
                "change": "add",
                "color": "purple",
                "bold": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "So the answer is 16 x to the 12, y to the 4.",
          "write": "= 16x¹²y⁴"
        },
        {
          "say": "This only works for products. x plus 2, squared, is not x squared plus 4.",
          "write": "(x + 2)² ≠ x² + 4"
        },
        {
          "say": "For fractions, raise the top and the bottom.",
          "write": "(x/2)³ = x³/8"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Same base only",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "These laws need the same base. a cubed times b squared can't simplify.",
          "write": "a³ × b² stays"
        },
        {
          "say": "The base never changes. 5 squared times 5 cubed is 5 to the 5.",
          "write": "5² × 5³ = 5⁵, not 25⁵",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 240,
            "alt": "Five squared times five cubed is five to the five, not twenty-five to the five",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 150,
                "y": 110,
                "text": "5² × 5³",
                "size": 40,
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 150,
                "y": 190,
                "text": "= 5⁵",
                "size": 40,
                "change": "add",
                "color": "green",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 420,
                "y": 190,
                "text": "25⁵",
                "size": 40,
                "change": "remove",
                "color": "red",
                "bold": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Sometimes bases are hidden. 8 is 2 cubed and 4 is 2 squared.",
          "write": "8 × 4² = 2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷"
        },
        {
          "say": "2 to the 7 is 128. Check: 8 times 16 is 128.",
          "write": "= 128 ✓"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Watch out for these",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "x cubed times x to the 5 is x to the 8, not x to the 15.",
          "write": "x³ × x⁵ = x⁸"
        },
        {
          "say": "Raise the number in the bracket too. 3 y squared, cubed, is 27 y to the 6.",
          "write": "(3y²)³ = 27y⁶"
        },
        {
          "say": "Don't add coefficients. 4 times 5 is 20, so 20 a to the 5.",
          "write": "4a² × 5a³ = 20a⁵"
        },
        {
          "say": "Don't miss the invisible 1. k to the 6 over k is k to the 5.",
          "write": "k⁶ ÷ k = k⁵"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Remember",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Multiply: add the indices. Divide: subtract them.",
          "write": "× add,  ÷ subtract"
        },
        {
          "say": "Power of a power: multiply the indices.",
          "write": "(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ"
        },
        {
          "say": "Brackets first, then numbers, then one letter at a time.",
          "write": "brackets → numbers → letters"
        },
        {
          "say": "Not sure? Check with a small number like 2.",
          "write": "check with 2"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
